{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1412617253,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE3NDY3NDQyNg=="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1412617253,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE3NDY3NDQyNw=="},"added":["annex"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1417493986,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDIwMTAyMzAxNA=="},"added":["! important !"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1417545180,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY1Mjc5NjQ2","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/10#issuecomment-65279646"},"message":"At least some of this sounds like it would be best handled by building an external special remote. http://git-annex.branchable.com/design/external_special_remote_protocol/\n\nThat seems more likely to handle some of the unusual use cases, rather than trying to shoe-horn everything through URL handling.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1417548919,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY1MjkwMDUw","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/10#issuecomment-65290050"},"message":"On Tue, 02 Dec 2014, Joey Hess wrote:\n\n\u003e    At least some of this sounds like it would be best handled by building an\n\u003e    external special remote.\n\u003e    http://git-annex.branchable.com/design/external_special_remote_protocol/\n\u003e    That seems more likely to handle some of the unusual use cases, rather\n\u003e    than trying to show-horn everything through URL handling.\n\nyes indeed, thanks for the reminder about this feature!  And it sounds\nlike there could also be a middle-case where we could provide local\nadapter which would serve as an \"external special remote\" e.g. in case\nof datasets served already from S3 buckets with versioning\nenabled.  There ETags correspond to md5sum's of the load, so if we keep a\ntable of translations from underlying annex backend (e.g. SHA256) to\nmd5sums (with hope of no overlaps, or adding size as an additional\ncheck), then such an adapter could request load from s3 buckets.  But I\nwonder if that would be beneficial over e.g. populating files with URLs\npointing to http frontend to S3 where we could request specific version\nof a file anyways?\n\n## \n\nYaroslav O. Halchenko, Ph.D.\nhttp://neuro.debian.net http://www.pymvpa.org http://www.fail2ban.org\nResearch Scientist,            Psychological and Brain Sciences Dept.\nDartmouth College, 419 Moore Hall, Hinman Box 6207, Hanover, NH 03755\nPhone: +1 (603) 646-9834                       Fax: +1 (603) 646-1419\nWWW:   http://www.linkedin.com/in/yarik","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1417637982,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY1NDgyNTc3","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/10#issuecomment-65482577"},"message":"Yaroslav Halchenko wrote:\n\n\u003e yes indeed, thanks for the reminder about this feature! And it sounds\n\u003e like there could also be a middle-case where we could provide local\n\u003e adapter which would serve as an \"external special remote\" e.g. in case\n\u003e of datasets served already from S3 buckets with versioning\n\u003e enabled. There ETags correspond to md5sum's of the load, so if we keep a\n\u003e table of translations from underlying annex backend (e.g. SHA256) to\n\u003e md5sums (with hope of no overlaps, or adding size as an additional\n\u003e check), then such an adapter could request load from s3 buckets. But I\n\u003e wonder if that would be beneficial over e.g. populating files with URLs\n\u003e pointing to http frontend to S3 where we could request specific version\n\u003e of a file anyways?\n\nThe S3 case seems just as well handled by `git annex addurl`, with an\nURL pointing at the right version of the file in the S3 bucket.\n\nExternal special remotes make sense for cases like extraction from\nlocally present archives. I think it also makes sense for cases where\na data source requires some credentials to use.\n\nAs for extending git-annex's url handling to support eg, .torrent or\nother pluggable extensions, I think there is room for a simple pluggable\ninterface there. Indeed, git-annex already has some basics toward one.\n\nWhat git-annex already has is that an url can be prefixed with\n\"quvi:\", and then git-annex knows to use quvi for that url. `git-annex addurl`\nchecks if the url is one quvi supports, and if so, records the url as\n\"quvi:url\". This way, git-annex always consistently downloads that url\nwith quvi (or fails if quvi is not installed).\n\nThat could be extended, by having some way to register a downloader.\nFor example:\n\n```\ngit config annex.downloader.torrent.command 'aria2c %url $file'\ngit config annex.downloader.torrent.regexp '(^magnet:|\\.torrent$)'\n```\n\nThen `git-annex addurl` would check if the url matches the regexp, and\nif so, record the url as (in the above example) \"torrent:$url\".\n\nThen when downloading \"torrent:$url\", git-annex would look for the\nappropriate annex.downloader.torrent.command and run it.\n\n(Torrent files are actually a difficult case, since an individual torrent\ncan contain multiple files, and git-annex expects to get exactly one\nfile when it downloads an url. So a torrent downloader would need to be\nused with only single-file torrents, or somehow pick out the file that\nis wanted from a multi-file torrent. Perhaps specified by appending\nsomething like '?wanted-file=foo' to the torrent's url.)\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1417638756,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY1NDg1MTUx","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/10#issuecomment-65485151"},"message":"Hmm, for magnet: torrents, it would also need a separate command to check if the torrent is still available (to the extent that can be checked at all for torrent swarms where peers come and go). Defaulting to just checking if the url is available seems reasonable in general though.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1417639577,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDY1NDg3NTY0","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/10#issuecomment-65487564"},"message":"Hmm, it kind of seems that torrents are sufficienty complicated that it would be better implemented inside git-annex (running aria2c with the correct options) than as a plug-in downloader.\n\nSo, some examples of other sorts of urls or uris that could be handled by this proposed downloader interface would be useful, so I can check if I'm being sufficiently general and know it would really be useful.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464710510,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDY3NzIyNTgzOA=="},"added":["do-in-datalad"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464710510,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50Njc3MjI1ODM5"},"added":[],"removed":["annex"]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1464710543,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyMjczNTg1Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/11#issuecomment-222735856"},"message":"well:\n- git annex supports parallel downloads now with `-J` switch, so kinda \"solved\" on annex side (removing git-annex label)\n- there is an outstanding issue to debug/fix for requesting multiple files from the same archive (#451)\n- our `install` commands allows for multiple targets for installation ATM, and the rest of the logic on analysis of what should be the most efficient 'annex get' operations would be is TODO.  See  https://git-annex.branchable.com/todo/wishlist__58___--dry-run_option_for_all_commands/  and the particular command would be `git annex find --not --in here -j [paths]` which would return in json records also the keys in question","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536843918,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMDk5OTM1Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/11#issuecomment-420999353"},"message":"I think it was largely solved, not clear what else we should possibly do here, thus closing","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536843918,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTg0MzU2NzQzNQ=="},"status":2}]}